  "history": "Azúcar has walked a long way. In Sanskrit, śarkarā meant grit, and also the grainy sugar from cane. Persian made it šakar, and in the Middle Ages Arabic made it sukkar. In al-Andalus people said as-sukkar, ‘the sugar’, and Spanish took the article with the noun, as it did with aceite. That a- at the start is Arabic ‘the’. Arabic then carried the word across Europe, by routes that gave French sucre, Italian zucchero and German Zucker. The Romans barely knew the thing: when Pliny writes saccharon, he means a sweet substance from bamboo. A grammar note: azúcar is masculine by origin, but the RAE accepts both azúcar moreno and azúcar morena.",
  "historyEinfach": "Azúcar is an old word from India. It meant ‘small stones’ and ‘sugar’. The word went to Persia and then to the Arabs. In Spain, Arabic speakers said as-sukkar, ‘the sugar’. Spanish made this one word: azúcar. So the a- at the start is the Arabic word for ‘the’. French sucre and German Zucker are the same word.",
